// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 15): technology.
ADDART({id:"usb",t:"USB and Plugs",c:"Technology",i:"🔌",w:"USB",r:["comp","battery","smartphone","personalcomputers","bluetooth"],
p:[["One plug for many devices","USB connects keyboards, phones, drives and chargers to computers."],["From many cables to one","It replaced a tangle of different connectors."],["USB-C everywhere","The reversible USB-C plug is now the standard for phones and laptops."]],
l:"USB, short for Universal Serial Bus, is a standard for connecting devices such as keyboards, mice, printers, cameras, flash drives and phones to computers and for charging them. Before USB, computers had many different ports, including serial, parallel and special game and keyboard connectors, and adding a device often meant restarting the computer and installing software. A group of companies, including Intel, Microsoft, IBM and Compaq, developed USB, with Intel engineer Ajay Bhatt among its leaders, and the first version was released in 1996. USB let devices be plugged in and unplugged while the computer was running and supplied power through the same cable. Later versions became much faster, and small USB flash drives, introduced around 2000, replaced floppy disks. The rectangular USB-A plug was famous for seeming to need three tries to insert. In 2014, the reversible USB-C connector was introduced, able to carry data, video and enough power to charge laptops. The European Union required new phones and many other devices sold there to use USB-C charging ports from December 2024, with laptops following in 2026, to reduce electronic waste; Apple switched its iPhones to USB-C in 2023.",
s:[["Plug and play","USB devices tell the computer what they are when connected, so the operating system can load the right software automatically, a feature called plug and play. This made adding devices much easier for ordinary users."],["Speeds","USB 1.0 transferred up to 12 megabits per second. USB 2.0 (2000) reached 480 megabits, USB 3.0 (2008) 5 gigabits, and USB4 versions reach 40 gigabits per second or more, fast enough for external screens and storage."],["Charging","USB Power Delivery lets USB-C cables carry up to 240 watts, enough for powerful laptops. Many countries and airlines now provide USB charging points, though security experts warn against plugging phones into unknown public ports."],["Common charger law","The European Union’s common charger rules aim to let people use one charger for many devices and buy devices without a new charger, reducing thousands of metric tons of e-waste each year."]],
k:[["Stands for","Universal Serial Bus"],["First version","1996"],["Flash drives","About 2000"],["USB-C introduced","2014"],["EU common charger rule for phones","December 2024"]],
d:[["1996","USB 1.0 released"],["2000","USB 2.0 and early USB flash drives"],["2008","USB 3.0 released"],["2014","USB-C connector introduced"],["2019","USB4 announced"],["2023","Apple switches iPhones to USB-C"],["2024","EU requires USB-C charging on new phones"]],
s2:[["The three-try plug","People joked that a USB-A plug needed three attempts: wrong way, wrong way again, then right. The symmetrical USB-C design ended the problem."],["Ajay Bhatt","Ajay Bhatt, an Indian-American engineer at Intel, became known as a ‘father of USB’ and was even featured in an Intel television advertisement in 2009 portraying him as a rock star."]],
sp:["The trident logo","The USB logo is based on the trident of Neptune, the Roman sea god, with a circle, triangle and square at the tips representing the many kinds of devices USB can connect."],
g:[["Port","A socket on a device for plugging in cables"],["Plug and play","Working automatically when connected"],["Flash drive","A small USB storage stick"],["USB-C","A reversible USB connector"]],
f:["USB was first released in 1996.","USB-C plugs work either way up.","The EU requires USB-C on new phones."],
fun:"The USB logo is based on the trident of the sea god Neptune.",
tl:"Pinagdurugtong ng USB ang keyboard, telepono, drive at charger sa computer. Pinalitan nito ang gulo ng iba’t ibang connector, at ang nababaligtad na USB-C na ang pamantayan para sa telepono at laptop."});

ADDART({id:"linux",t:"Linux",c:"Technology",i:"🐧",w:"Linux",r:["operatingsystems","opensource","comp","supercomputers","smartphone"],
p:[["A free operating system","Linux is a free, open-source operating system started in 1991."],["Started by a student","Finnish student Linus Torvalds created it as a hobby."],["Running the world","Linux runs Android phones, most servers and all top supercomputers."]],
l:"Linux is a family of free and open-source operating systems built around the Linux kernel, the core program that manages a computer’s hardware. On 25 August 1991, Linus Torvalds, a 21-year-old computer science student at the University of Helsinki in Finland, announced on an internet discussion group that he was making a free operating system ‘just a hobby, won’t be big and professional’. Combined with tools from the GNU Project, started by programmer Richard Stallman in 1983 to create a free Unix-like system, the Linux kernel formed a complete operating system. Because its source code is published under the GNU General Public License, anyone can study, change and share it, and thousands of volunteers and companies contribute. Linux is packaged in versions called distributions, such as Debian, Ubuntu, Fedora and Red Hat Enterprise Linux. Although Linux is used on only a small share of desktop computers, it dominates elsewhere: it runs most web servers and cloud computing, every one of the world’s 500 fastest supercomputers, and Android, the most widely used smartphone operating system. It also powers televisions, cars, routers, the Raspberry Pi and even NASA’s Ingenuity helicopter on Mars.",
s:[["Open source","Linux is a leading example of open-source software, developed openly by a global community. Torvalds still oversees the kernel, accepting changes from thousands of developers, many working for companies such as Intel, Google and Red Hat."],["Distributions","A Linux distribution bundles the kernel with software, a desktop and tools for installing programs. Some aim to be easy for beginners, while others suit servers, security experts or very old computers."],["Tux","The Linux mascot is a cheerful penguin named Tux, drawn by Larry Ewing in 1996. Torvalds said he liked penguins and had once been nipped by one at a zoo in Australia."],["Git","In 2005, Torvalds also created Git, a system for tracking changes in code, to manage Linux development. Git became the most widely used version control system in the world, the basis of sites such as GitHub."]],
k:[["Announced","25 August 1991"],["Creator","Linus Torvalds"],["License","GNU General Public License"],["Mascot","Tux the penguin"],["Major use","Servers, Android, supercomputers"]],
d:[["1983","Richard Stallman launches the GNU Project"],["1991","Linus Torvalds announces Linux"],["1994","Linux 1.0 released"],["1996","Tux the penguin becomes the mascot"],["2004","Ubuntu first released"],["2008","First Android phone, built on Linux, goes on sale"],["2017","Linux runs all of the top 500 supercomputers"]],
s2:[["Linux on Mars","NASA’s Ingenuity helicopter, which made the first powered flight on another planet in 2021, ran its flight software on Linux, using open-source code."],["The name","Torvalds originally wanted to call his system ‘Freax’, but the administrator of the server where it was first shared named the folder ‘linux’, and the name stuck."]],
sp:["Just a hobby","Torvalds’s 1991 announcement predicted his system would never be big. Today, Linux runs on billions of devices."],
g:[["Kernel","The core of an operating system"],["Open source","Software whose code anyone can see and change"],["Distribution","A packaged version of Linux"],["Server","A computer that provides services to others"]],
f:["Linux was announced in 1991.","Its mascot is Tux the penguin.","Android is built on Linux."],
fun:"Linus Torvalds described Linux as ‘just a hobby’ when he announced it, and it now runs on billions of devices.",
tl:"Ang Linux ay libre at open-source na operating system na sinimulan noong 1991 ng Finnish na estudyanteng si Linus Torvalds. Pinatatakbo nito ang mga Android phone, karamihan ng server at lahat ng nangungunang supercomputer."});

ADDART({id:"compression",t:"Data Compression",c:"Technology",i:"🗜️",w:"Data compression",r:["datastorage","streaming","soundrecording","photography","binary"],
p:[["Making files smaller","Compression shrinks data so it takes less space and travels faster."],["Lossless and lossy","Some methods keep every bit; others throw away details we barely notice."],["JPEG and MP3","Compressed formats made digital photos, music and video practical."]],
l:"Data compression is the technique of encoding information using fewer bits than the original, so that files take up less storage space and can be sent faster over networks. Lossless compression, used for documents, programs and some images, lets the original be restored exactly by finding and removing repetition; for example, a long run of identical pixels can be stored as a count. Huffman coding, invented by David Huffman in 1952, gives common symbols shorter codes, an idea also used in Morse code, and the Lempel–Ziv methods of 1977 and 1978 underlie ZIP files and PNG images. Lossy compression, used for photos, music and video, discards details that human eyes and ears are unlikely to notice, achieving much smaller files. The JPEG image standard of 1992 made digital photography and web images practical, and the MP3 audio format, developed mainly at Germany’s Fraunhofer Institute, could shrink music files to about a tenth of their size, sparking the digital music revolution. Video formats such as H.264, released in 2003, and newer ones such as HEVC and AV1 make streaming possible; without compression, an hour of high-definition video would need hundreds of gigabytes.",
s:[["How lossless works","Lossless methods replace repeated patterns with shorter references. Text often compresses to less than half its size. Already-compressed files, such as JPEGs, shrink little more, because their repetition has been removed."],["How lossy works","Lossy image formats divide pictures into small blocks and keep the broad patterns while discarding fine detail. MP3 uses psychoacoustics, the study of hearing, to remove sounds masked by louder sounds. Heavy compression can cause visible blocks or ‘tinny’ sound."],["The MP3","Engineer Karlheinz Brandenburg and colleagues at Fraunhofer developed MP3 in the late 1980s and early 1990s. The file extension .mp3 was chosen in July 1995. MP3s fueled music players, file-sharing services such as Napster and, eventually, streaming."],["Video","Video compression saves only the changes between frames where possible, because much of a scene stays the same from one frame to the next. Streaming services choose the quality automatically based on the viewer’s internet speed."]],
k:[["Huffman coding","1952"],["Lempel–Ziv methods","1977–1978"],["JPEG standard","1992"],["MP3 name chosen","1995"],["Common video format","H.264 (2003)"]],
d:[["1838","Morse code uses short signals for common letters"],["1952","Huffman coding invented"],["1977","First Lempel–Ziv algorithm"],["1989","ZIP file format created"],["1992","JPEG standard published"],["1995","The .mp3 name is chosen"],["2003","H.264 video standard released"]],
s2:[["Tom’s Diner","Karlheinz Brandenburg tested MP3 repeatedly using Suzanne Vega’s a cappella song ‘Tom’s Diner’, because a solo human voice made flaws easy to hear. Vega has been called the ‘mother of the MP3’."],["Morse code","Samuel Morse and Alfred Vail gave the most common English letter, E, the shortest code, a single dot, an early example of the idea behind compression."]],
sp:["Too big without it","An uncompressed 4K movie could take several terabytes. Compression lets it stream over an ordinary home internet connection."],
g:[["Compression","Encoding data in fewer bits"],["Lossless","Compression that keeps every detail"],["Lossy","Compression that discards some detail"],["Codec","Software that compresses and decompresses media"]],
f:["Lossless compression keeps every bit.","JPEG was standardized in 1992.","MP3 made music files about ten times smaller."],
fun:"Suzanne Vega’s song ‘Tom’s Diner’ was used again and again to test the MP3 format, earning her the nickname ‘mother of the MP3’.",
tl:"Pinaliliit ng data compression ang mga file upang makatipid sa espasyo at mas mabilis maipadala. Pinananatili ng ilang paraan ang bawat bit habang tinatanggal ng iba ang detalyeng halos hindi napapansin, at pinadali ng JPEG at MP3 ang digital na larawan at musika."});

ADDART({id:"instantcameras",t:"Instant Cameras",c:"Technology",i:"📸",w:"Instant camera",r:["camera","photography","reactions","light","videotape"],
p:[["Photos in minutes","Instant cameras print a developed photo moments after it is taken."],["A child’s question","Edwin Land invented them after his daughter asked why she could not see a photo right away."],["Back in fashion","Instant cameras are popular again in the digital age."]],
l:"An instant camera produces a finished, printed photograph within minutes of the picture being taken, using film packets that contain their own developing chemicals. The idea came from American scientist Edwin Land, founder of the Polaroid Corporation. In 1943, while on vacation, his three-year-old daughter asked why she could not see the photo he had just taken. Land worked out how to do it, and on 26 November 1948 the first Polaroid Land Camera went on sale at a department store in Boston, selling out on its first day. Early instant photos were sepia or black and white; color instant film arrived in 1963. The SX-70 of 1972 produced self-developing prints that emerged from the camera and gradually appeared before the user’s eyes. Instant photos became part of popular culture, used at parties, by artists such as Andy Warhol and by police and insurance agents. After digital cameras took over, Polaroid went bankrupt and stopped making film in 2008, but enthusiasts revived production through the Impossible Project. Today, Fujifilm’s Instax cameras, launched in 1998, are best-sellers, and many young people enjoy instant photos as physical keepsakes.",
s:[["How it works","Instant film contains layers of light-sensitive chemicals and a pod of developing paste. When the photo is ejected, rollers burst the pod and spread the paste across the image, starting chemical reactions that develop the picture in a few minutes."],["Shaking the photo","Many people shake instant photos to help them develop, but Polaroid says this is unnecessary and can even smear modern prints, which develop best lying flat and shielded from light."],["Polaroid vs Kodak","Kodak launched its own instant cameras in 1976. Polaroid sued for patent infringement and won in 1986; Kodak left the instant camera business and later paid Polaroid about 925 million dollars, one of the largest patent settlements of its time."],["The revival","In 2008, a group led by Florian Kaps bought Polaroid’s last factory in the Netherlands and reinvented the film, calling themselves the Impossible Project. The company later took the Polaroid name."]],
k:[["Inventor","Edwin Land"],["First camera on sale","26 November 1948"],["Color instant film","1963"],["Self-developing SX-70","1972"],["Popular brand today","Fujifilm Instax"]],
d:[["1937","Edwin Land founds Polaroid"],["1943","Land’s daughter asks why photos take so long"],["1948","First Polaroid camera sold"],["1972","SX-70 camera introduced"],["1986","Polaroid wins its patent case against Kodak"],["1998","Fujifilm launches Instax"],["2008","Polaroid stops making film; Impossible Project begins"]],
s2:[["Edwin Land","Land, who left Harvard without graduating, held more than 500 patents, second only to Thomas Edison in the United States at the time. He also invented polarizing filters used in sunglasses and 3D glasses."],["Art and instant photos","Artists such as Andy Warhol, Ansel Adams and David Hockney used Polaroid cameras. Hockney made collages from dozens of Polaroid prints."]],
sp:["Land the adviser","Besides cameras, Edwin Land advised several US presidents on science and helped plan early spy satellite and reconnaissance aircraft projects."],
g:[["Instant camera","A camera that prints developed photos quickly"],["Developing","Chemically making a photo image appear"],["Film pack","A cartridge of instant film sheets"],["Patent","A legal right to an invention"]],
f:["The first instant camera went on sale in 1948.","Edwin Land founded Polaroid.","Shaking an instant photo does not help it develop."],
fun:"The instant camera was invented because a three-year-old girl asked why she couldn’t see her photo right away.",
tl:"Nagpi-print ang instant camera ng nadebelop nang larawan ilang sandali matapos itong kunan. Inimbento ito ni Edwin Land matapos itanong ng kanyang anak kung bakit hindi agad makita ang larawan, at muling sumikat ang mga ito sa panahong digital."});

ADDART({id:"videotape",t:"Videotape and VHS",c:"Technology",i:"📼",w:"VHS",r:["tv","soundrecording","datastorage","cinema","streaming"],
p:[["Recording TV","Videotape recorded moving pictures and sound on magnetic tape."],["The format war","VHS beat Sony’s Betamax in the 1980s."],["Movies at home","Video rental stores let families watch films at home."]],
l:"Videotape is magnetic tape used to record television pictures and sound. Before it, television programs were broadcast live or recorded on film. In 1956, the American company Ampex demonstrated the first practical videotape recorder, a machine the size of a large desk using tape 5 centimeters wide, and television networks quickly adopted it so programs could be recorded and replayed. Home video recorders followed in the 1970s. Sony introduced Betamax in 1975, and the Japanese company JVC launched the rival Video Home System (VHS) in 1976. The two incompatible formats fought a ‘format war’; although many thought Betamax had better picture quality, VHS tapes could record longer, machines were cheaper and more companies made them, so VHS won by the late 1980s. Videocassette recorders (VCRs) let people record TV shows to watch later, rent movies from video stores such as Blockbuster and make home movies with camcorders. In 1984, the US Supreme Court ruled in the ‘Betamax case’ that recording TV programs at home for later viewing was legal. DVDs, introduced in the late 1990s, then streaming replaced videotape, and the last VCRs were made in 2016.",
s:[["How it works","Video signals contain far more information than sound, so videotape recorders use spinning heads that record diagonal stripes across the tape, packing in more information than a fixed head could. A cassette protects the tape and makes loading easy."],["The format war","Sony kept Betamax largely to itself, while JVC licensed VHS to many manufacturers. VHS tapes could hold a full-length movie from the start, which helped video rental stores choose VHS. The battle became a classic business case study."],["Video stores","Video rental stores spread worldwide in the 1980s. Blockbuster, founded in 1985, grew to thousands of stores before streaming and mail-order DVD services drove it out of business; a single Blockbuster store survives in Bend, Oregon."],["Be kind, rewind","Rental stores asked customers to rewind tapes before returning them, a phrase printed on stickers. Some charged fees for unrewound tapes."]],
k:[["First practical video recorder","Ampex, 1956"],["Betamax","Sony, 1975"],["VHS","JVC, 1976"],["Betamax case","US Supreme Court, 1984"],["Last VCRs made","2016"]],
d:[["1956","Ampex demonstrates the videotape recorder"],["1971","Sony U-matic videocassette"],["1975","Betamax launched"],["1976","VHS launched"],["1984","Supreme Court rules home recording legal"],["1997","DVD players go on sale in the US"],["2016","Last VCRs manufactured"]],
s2:[["Camcorders","Camcorders combined a video camera and recorder in one device from the 1980s, letting families film birthdays and holidays. Many old home videos are now being digitized before the tapes deteriorate."],["Saving tape","Magnetic tapes slowly degrade, and players are disappearing. Archives around the world are racing to digitize historic videotapes, including TV news and cultural recordings."]],
sp:["The last Blockbuster","The last remaining Blockbuster video store, in Bend, Oregon, became a tourist attraction and even appeared in a 2020 documentary."],
g:[["Videotape","Magnetic tape that records video"],["VCR","Videocassette recorder"],["Format war","A battle between incompatible technologies"],["Camcorder","A portable video camera and recorder"]],
f:["Ampex demonstrated videotape in 1956.","VHS beat Betamax.","The last VCRs were made in 2016."],
fun:"Video rental stores asked customers to ‘be kind, rewind’ their tapes before returning them.",
tl:"Nagtatala ang videotape ng gumagalaw na larawan at tunog sa magnetic tape. Tinalo ng VHS ang Betamax ng Sony noong dekada 1980, at nakapanood ang mga pamilya ng pelikula sa bahay dahil sa mga video rental store."});

ADDART({id:"gameconsoles",t:"Video Game Consoles",c:"Technology",i:"🎮",w:"Video game console",r:["videogames","arcadegames","gpus","japancountry","esports"],
p:[["Games on the TV","Consoles are computers built for playing games on a television."],["From Odyssey to Switch","Consoles evolved from simple dots to lifelike worlds."],["Nintendo, Sony and Microsoft","Three companies dominate the console market."]],
l:"A video game console is a computer designed mainly for playing video games, usually connected to a television or built into a handheld device. The first home console, the Magnavox Odyssey, designed by engineer Ralph Baer, went on sale in 1972 and showed simple white shapes on screen. The Atari 2600 of 1977 popularized game cartridges, letting players swap games. After an oversupply of poor-quality games caused the North American video game crash of 1983, Nintendo revived the industry with the Family Computer (Famicom) in Japan in 1983, released elsewhere as the Nintendo Entertainment System, featuring Super Mario Bros. Nintendo’s handheld Game Boy (1989) sold more than 100 million units. Sony’s PlayStation, released in 1994, brought 3D graphics and CDs, and the PlayStation 2 became one of the best-selling consoles ever. Microsoft entered with the Xbox in 2001. Nintendo’s Wii (2006) attracted new players with motion controls, and its Switch (2017), which works both as a home and handheld console, became one of the most successful consoles in history; its successor, the Switch 2, launched in June 2025. Consoles now offer online play, streaming and digital game stores.",
s:[["Console generations","Consoles are grouped into generations. Early ones had 8-bit processors and simple sound; later generations brought 3D graphics, discs, high definition, online gaming and, more recently, 4K graphics and fast solid-state storage."],["The 1983 crash","A flood of low-quality games and competition from home computers caused North American console sales to collapse in 1983. Thousands of unsold Atari cartridges, including the poorly received E.T. game, were buried in a New Mexico landfill and dug up in 2014."],["Handhelds","Handheld consoles from the Game Boy to the Nintendo DS and PlayStation Portable let people play anywhere. Smartphones have since become the most popular way to play games worldwide."],["Console wars","Rivalries between Nintendo and Sega in the early 1990s, and later between Sony, Microsoft and Nintendo, drove innovation and memorable advertising campaigns."]],
k:[["First home console","Magnavox Odyssey, 1972"],["Famicom/NES","1983 (Japan)"],["PlayStation","1994"],["Xbox","2001"],["Nintendo Switch 2","June 2025"]],
d:[["1972","Magnavox Odyssey released"],["1977","Atari 2600 released"],["1983","North American video game crash; Famicom launched in Japan"],["1989","Game Boy released"],["1994","Sony PlayStation released in Japan"],["2001","Microsoft Xbox released"],["2017","Nintendo Switch released"],["2025","Nintendo Switch 2 released"]],
s2:[["Ralph Baer","Ralph Baer, a German-born American engineer, began designing a game machine for televisions in 1966. Called the ‘father of video games’, he also co-invented the electronic memory game Simon."],["Tetris","Tetris, created by Soviet programmer Alexey Pajitnov in 1984, was bundled with the Game Boy and became one of the best-selling games of all time."]],
sp:["The buried cartridges","In 2014, filmmakers excavated a New Mexico landfill and found the long-rumored buried Atari cartridges, confirming a legend from the 1983 crash."],
g:[["Console","A computer built for playing games"],["Cartridge","A plug-in game memory module"],["Handheld","A portable game device"],["Generation","A group of consoles from the same era"]],
f:["The first home console was released in 1972.","The Game Boy came out in 1989.","The Switch 2 launched in 2025."],
fun:"Thousands of unsold Atari game cartridges were buried in a New Mexico landfill in 1983 and dug up in 2014.",
tl:"Ang game console ay computer na ginawa para sa paglalaro sa telebisyon. Mula Odyssey hanggang Switch, umunlad ang mga console mula sa simpleng tuldok hanggang sa makatotohanang mundo, at tatlong kompanya ang nangunguna sa merkado."});

ADDART({id:"supersonic",t:"Concorde and Supersonic Flight",c:"Technology",i:"✈️",w:"Concorde",r:["flight","jetengine","sound","airports","airships"],
p:[["Faster than sound","Supersonic aircraft fly faster than the speed of sound."],["Concorde","The Anglo-French Concorde carried passengers at twice the speed of sound."],["Quieter booms","New aircraft aim to bring back supersonic travel with softer sonic booms."]],
l:"Supersonic flight is flight faster than the speed of sound, about 1,230 kilometers per hour at sea level, a speed called Mach 1. On 14 October 1947, US Air Force pilot Chuck Yeager became the first person confirmed to break the sound barrier in level flight, in the rocket-powered Bell X-1. Supersonic military jets followed. Two supersonic airliners entered service: the Soviet Tupolev Tu-144, which first flew on 31 December 1968 but carried passengers only briefly, and Concorde, built jointly by Britain and France, which first flew on 2 March 1969 and began passenger service on 21 January 1976. Concorde cruised at about Mach 2, more than twice the speed of sound, at altitudes of up to 18,000 meters, crossing the Atlantic from London or Paris to New York in about three and a half hours. However, it was very expensive, burned huge amounts of fuel and created sonic booms, so it was banned from supersonic flight over land and only 14 entered airline service. After the crash of Air France Flight 4590 near Paris on 25 July 2000, which killed 113 people, and a downturn in air travel, Concorde was retired in 2003. Companies and NASA are now developing quieter supersonic aircraft.",
s:[["Sonic booms","An aircraft flying faster than sound creates shock waves that spread out in a cone behind it. When the cone passes over the ground, people hear a loud double bang. Because of this, the United States banned civilian supersonic flight over land in 1973."],["Concorde’s design","Concorde had slender delta wings, a nose that drooped down for takeoff and landing so pilots could see the runway, and powerful engines with afterburners. Friction heating at Mach 2 made the plane stretch by about 15 to 25 centimeters in flight."],["New supersonic aircraft","Boom Supersonic’s XB-1 demonstrator broke the sound barrier on 28 January 2025, and NASA’s X-59, designed to make a quiet ‘thump’ instead of a boom, first flew on 28 October 2025. Data from the X-59 may help regulators reconsider bans on supersonic flight over land."],["Faster than the Sun","Flying westward in winter, Concorde could leave London after sunset and arrive in New York while it was still daylight, seeming to make the Sun rise in the west."]],
k:[["Speed of sound at sea level","About 1,230 km/h (Mach 1)"],["First to break the sound barrier","Chuck Yeager, 14 October 1947"],["Concorde first flight","2 March 1969"],["Concorde passenger service","1976–2003"],["Cruising speed","About Mach 2"]],
d:[["1947","Chuck Yeager breaks the sound barrier"],["1968","Tu-144 first flight"],["1969","Concorde first flight"],["1976","Concorde passenger service begins"],["2000","Air France Concorde crash"],["2003","Concorde retired"],["2025","Boom XB-1 breaks the sound barrier; NASA X-59 first flight"]],
s2:[["Fastest crossing","On 7 February 1996, a British Airways Concorde flew from New York to London in 2 hours, 52 minutes and 59 seconds, the fastest transatlantic crossing by an airliner."],["Where to see Concorde","Concordes are displayed in museums in Britain, France, the United States and Barbados, where visitors can walk through their narrow cabins."]],
sp:["Champagne and caviar","Concorde passengers, often business travelers and celebrities, paid thousands of dollars for tickets and enjoyed luxury meals, but the cabin was narrow, with small windows the size of a hand."],
g:[["Supersonic","Faster than the speed of sound"],["Mach number","Speed compared with the speed of sound"],["Sonic boom","A loud noise from shock waves of supersonic flight"],["Delta wing","A triangle-shaped wing"]],
f:["Chuck Yeager broke the sound barrier in 1947.","Concorde flew at about twice the speed of sound.","Concorde was retired in 2003."],
fun:"Concorde grew longer by up to about 25 centimeters in flight as friction heated its body.",
tl:"Lumilipad nang mas mabilis kaysa tunog ang mga supersonic na eroplano. Nagdala ang Concorde ng mga pasahero sa dalawang ulit ng bilis ng tunog, at naglalayon ang mga bagong eroplano na ibalik ang supersonic na biyahe nang may mas mahinang sonic boom."});

ADDART({id:"airtaxis",t:"Air Taxis and Flying Cars",c:"Technology",i:"🚁",w:"Electric vertical take-off and landing",r:["helicopters","drones","battery","ev","airtrafficcontrol"],
p:[["Electric aircraft for cities","Air taxis are small electric aircraft that take off and land vertically."],["Many rotors","They use several electric rotors, like large drones."],["Starting soon","Companies are preparing passenger services in a few cities."]],
l:"Air taxis are small aircraft designed to carry a few passengers on short trips within and between cities, taking off and landing vertically like helicopters. Most are electric vertical take-off and landing (eVTOL) aircraft, which use several battery-powered rotors or tilting propellers instead of one large rotor and a fuel-burning engine. Supporters say they could be quieter, cleaner and cheaper to run than helicopters, letting travelers skip traffic jams; trips might take minutes instead of an hour. The dream of flying cars is old: inventors built roadable aircraft from the early 20th century, and science fiction often imagined skies full of personal vehicles. Advances in batteries, electric motors and drone control software made modern eVTOLs possible in the 2010s. Companies such as Joby Aviation and Archer Aviation in the United States, EHang in China, and others in Europe, Japan and Brazil have built prototypes and flown passengers on test flights. In 2023, EHang’s two-seat autonomous EH216-S became the first eVTOL to receive a type certificate from Chinese regulators. Companies aim to begin regular passenger services in cities such as Dubai, Abu Dhabi and New York, but certification, battery limits, noise, cost and air traffic management remain challenges.",
s:[["How eVTOLs fly","Many eVTOLs tilt their rotors upward to take off vertically, then forward to cruise on wings like an airplane, which is more efficient. Others use many fixed rotors, like a giant drone. Electric motors are simple and can be controlled precisely by computers."],["Vertiports","Air taxis need landing sites called vertiports, which could be built on rooftops, parking garages or near airports, with chargers for quick battery top-ups between flights."],["Challenges","Batteries store far less energy per kilogram than jet fuel, limiting range to roughly 100 to 250 kilometers. Regulators must certify the aircraft as safe as airliners, and cities must manage noise and many more aircraft in low airspace."],["Autonomy","Most early air taxis will have pilots, but some, such as EHang’s, are designed to fly without anyone at the controls, guided by computers and remote supervisors."]],
k:[["Type","Electric vertical take-off and landing (eVTOL)"],["Typical passengers","1–4 plus pilot"],["First eVTOL type certificate","EHang EH216-S, China, 2023"],["Key companies","Joby, Archer, EHang and others"],["Main challenge","Battery energy, certification, noise"]],
d:[["1917","Glenn Curtiss shows the Autoplane, an early flying car"],["1949","Taylor Aerocar roadable airplane flies"],["2010s","Electric multirotor aircraft developed"],["2023","EHang EH216-S certified in China"],["2026","Passenger services prepared in the Middle East and the United States"]],
s2:[["Flying cars in fiction","The 1960s cartoon The Jetsons and films such as Blade Runner and Back to the Future Part II pictured cities with flying cars, shaping public expectations."],["Air ambulances","Electric vertical aircraft could also carry medical supplies, organs for transplant and patients, and deliver aid after disasters."]],
sp:["Paris Olympics","Organizers hoped to fly air taxis during the 2024 Paris Olympics, but certification was not completed in time, showing how hard it is to bring new aircraft into service."],
g:[["eVTOL","Electric vertical take-off and landing aircraft"],["Vertiport","A landing site for vertical aircraft"],["Type certificate","Official approval of an aircraft design"],["Tilt-rotor","A rotor that can tilt between vertical and forward"]],
f:["Air taxis take off and land vertically.","Most are battery powered.","China certified the first eVTOL type in 2023."],
fun:"The dream of flying cars is more than a century old; Glenn Curtiss showed an ‘Autoplane’ in 1917.",
tl:"Ang air taxi ay maliliit na de-kuryenteng sasakyang panghimpapawid na lumilipad at lumalapag nang patayo. Gumagamit ito ng maraming de-kuryenteng rotor gaya ng malalaking drone, at naghahanda ang mga kompanya ng serbisyo para sa pasahero sa ilang lungsod."});

ADDART({id:"bci",t:"Brain–Computer Interfaces",c:"Technology",i:"🧠",w:"Brain–computer interface",r:["brain","nervous","ai","prosthetics","hearingaids"],
p:[["Thought into action","Brain–computer interfaces let people control devices with brain signals."],["Helping paralyzed people","They help people who cannot move or speak communicate."],["Implants and caps","Some use implanted electrodes; others use sensors on the scalp."]],
l:"A brain–computer interface (BCI) is a system that reads signals from the brain and translates them into commands for a computer, robotic arm or other device, letting people control technology with their thoughts. The brain’s nerve cells communicate with tiny electrical signals; in 1924, German psychiatrist Hans Berger first recorded brain waves from the scalp using electroencephalography (EEG). In 1973, computer scientist Jacques Vidal coined the term ‘brain–computer interface’. Non-invasive BCIs use EEG caps, which are safe but pick up blurry signals. Invasive BCIs place electrodes on or in the brain, recording individual nerve cells far more clearly. In the BrainGate trials, beginning in 2004, people with paralysis used implanted sensors to move computer cursors and later robotic arms. In 2023, researchers showed implants that let people who had lost the ability to speak produce words on screen or through a digital voice at close to conversational speed. Companies such as Synchron, which threads a sensor through a blood vessel, and Elon Musk’s Neuralink, which first implanted a device in a human in January 2024 and had 21 trial participants by early 2026, aim to make BCIs widely available. Ethical concerns include safety, privacy of brain data and fair access.",
s:[["How BCIs work","Electrodes detect electrical activity when a person imagines moving a hand or speaking. Machine-learning software learns which patterns correspond to which intentions and converts them into commands. Users and software improve together with practice."],["Restoring movement and speech","BCIs have let paralyzed people type, play video games, control wheelchairs and robotic arms and, in some experiments, move their own limbs by stimulating muscles or the spinal cord. Speech BCIs decode attempted speech from the brain’s speech areas."],["Other uses","Cochlear implants, which send sound signals to the hearing nerve, and deep brain stimulators for Parkinson’s disease are related technologies that interact with the nervous system. Consumer EEG headbands are sold for meditation and games, though their abilities are limited."],["Ethics","Experts debate who owns brain data, how to protect it from misuse and whether BCIs could ever read private thoughts. Chile became the first country to protect ‘neurorights’ in its constitution in 2021."]],
k:[["First human EEG","Hans Berger, 1924"],["Term coined","Jacques Vidal, 1973"],["BrainGate trials","Since 2004"],["Neuralink first human implant","January 2024"],["Main users","People with paralysis"]],
d:[["1924","First human EEG recording"],["1973","Term ‘brain–computer interface’ coined"],["2004","BrainGate trial begins"],["2012","Woman with paralysis controls a robotic arm to drink coffee"],["2021","Chile protects neurorights in its constitution"],["2023","Speech BCIs reach near-conversational speed"],["2024","Neuralink’s first human implant"]],
s2:[["A cup of coffee","In 2012, Cathy Hutchinson, paralyzed by a stroke, used a BrainGate implant to control a robotic arm and drink coffee by herself for the first time in nearly 15 years."],["Monkeys first","Before human trials, researchers trained monkeys with implants to move cursors and robotic arms, showing that brain signals could control machines in real time."]],
sp:["Playing chess by thought","Neuralink’s first participant, Noland Arbaugh, paralyzed from the shoulders down, used his implant to play chess and video games on a computer by thinking about moving the cursor."],
g:[["BCI","Brain–computer interface"],["Electrode","A conductor that picks up electrical signals"],["EEG","Recording brain waves from the scalp"],["Neurorights","Rights protecting brain data and mental privacy"]],
f:["Brain waves were first recorded in 1924.","BCIs help paralyzed people communicate.","Chile protects neurorights in its constitution."],
fun:"In 2012, a paralyzed woman drank coffee by herself using a robotic arm controlled by her thoughts.",
tl:"Pinapayagan ng brain–computer interface ang mga tao na kontrolin ang mga device gamit ang signal ng utak. Tinutulungan nito ang mga taong hindi makagalaw o makapagsalita, at may gumagamit ng implant habang ang iba ay sensor sa anit."});

ADDART({id:"roboticsurgery",t:"Robotic Surgery",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🦾",w:"Robot-assisted surgery",r:["surgery","robot","medimaging","telemedicine","hospitals"],
p:[["Robots in the operating room","Surgeons use robotic arms to perform precise operations."],["Small cuts","Robotic surgery uses tiny incisions instead of large openings."],["The surgeon is in control","Robots follow the surgeon’s hand movements."]],
l:"Robotic surgery, or robot-assisted surgery, is a way of performing operations in which a surgeon controls robotic arms holding tiny instruments and a camera, usually inserted through small cuts in the body. Sitting at a console, the surgeon views a magnified 3D image of the inside of the patient and moves hand controls; the robot translates these into precise movements, filtering out hand tremors and allowing instruments to bend and rotate more than a human wrist. The first robot-assisted operation is often traced to 1985, when an industrial robot arm called the PUMA 560 guided a needle for a brain biopsy. The most widely used system, the da Vinci Surgical System, was cleared by the US Food and Drug Administration in 2000 and has been used in more than 10 million procedures, especially prostate, gynecological, kidney and abdominal surgery. On 7 September 2001, surgeons in New York removed the gallbladder of a patient in Strasbourg, France, using a robot, the first transatlantic operation. Robotic surgery can mean smaller scars, less blood loss and faster recovery, but systems are very expensive, require special training and do not always produce better results than traditional methods.",
s:[["Minimally invasive surgery","Like keyhole (laparoscopic) surgery, robotic surgery uses small incisions instead of opening the body widely. The robot’s flexible wrists and steady movements make difficult tasks, such as stitching deep inside the pelvis, easier."],["Not autonomous","Today’s surgical robots do not operate on their own; they are tools controlled by surgeons. Researchers have tested more autonomous systems, such as a robot that stitched pig intestines in 2022 with little human help."],["Remote surgery","Fast networks could let expert surgeons operate on patients far away. Experiments in China and elsewhere have used 5G networks for remote operations, though delays in the signal are a serious risk."],["Costs and training","A surgical robot can cost more than a million dollars plus expensive instruments and maintenance. Surgeons practice on simulators before operating on patients."]],
k:[["Early robot-assisted procedure","PUMA 560 brain biopsy, 1985"],["da Vinci system cleared (US)","2000"],["Transatlantic operation","7 September 2001"],["Procedures with da Vinci","More than 10 million"],["Benefits","Small incisions, precision, faster recovery"]],
d:[["1985","PUMA 560 used for a brain biopsy"],["1990s","Early surgical robots developed"],["2000","da Vinci system cleared in the US"],["2001","First transatlantic remote operation"],["2022","Robot performs largely autonomous intestinal surgery on pigs"]],
s2:[["Operation Lindbergh","The 2001 transatlantic operation was nicknamed Operation Lindbergh, after Charles Lindbergh, who made the first solo nonstop flight across the Atlantic in 1927."],["Named after Leonardo","The da Vinci system is named after Leonardo da Vinci, who sketched anatomy and designed a mechanical knight, an early robot, around 1495."]],
sp:["Grape surgery","In a famous demonstration, surgeons used a da Vinci robot to peel the skin off a grape and stitch it back together, showing its delicate precision."],
g:[["Robot-assisted surgery","Surgery using robots controlled by surgeons"],["Incision","A surgical cut"],["Console","The control station where the surgeon sits"],["Laparoscopic surgery","Keyhole surgery using a camera"]],
f:["The da Vinci robot was cleared in 2000.","The first transatlantic operation was in 2001.","Surgeons control the robots."],
fun:"Surgeons once demonstrated a surgical robot by peeling a grape and stitching its skin back together.",
tl:"Gumagamit ang mga siruhano ng robotic arm upang magsagawa ng tumpak na operasyon. Maliliit na hiwa lamang ang ginagamit ng robotic surgery, at sinusunod ng robot ang galaw ng kamay ng siruhano."});

ADDART({id:"defibrillators",t:"Defibrillators",c:"Health and the Body",i:"❤️",w:"Defibrillation",r:["heart","firstaid","circuits","pacemakers","hospitals"],
p:[["Restarting the heart’s rhythm","Defibrillators use an electric shock to stop a dangerous heart rhythm."],["Anyone can use one","Automated external defibrillators (AEDs) talk users through each step."],["Every minute counts","Quick CPR and a shock greatly improve survival."]],
l:"A defibrillator is a device that delivers an electric shock to the heart to stop life-threatening irregular rhythms, especially ventricular fibrillation, in which the heart quivers instead of pumping blood. The shock briefly stops all electrical activity, allowing the heart’s natural pacemaker to restart a normal rhythm. Scientists had shocked animal hearts since the late 19th century, and in 1947, American surgeon Claude Beck used a defibrillator to save a 14-year-old boy whose heart fibrillated during surgery, the first successful human case. In 1965, Northern Irish doctor Frank Pantridge developed a portable defibrillator for ambulances in Belfast, bringing treatment to patients outside hospitals. Automated external defibrillators (AEDs), which analyze the heart’s rhythm themselves and tell users what to do with voice instructions, spread from the late 1970s and are now found in airports, schools, malls and sports stadiums. Implantable cardioverter-defibrillators (ICDs), small devices placed under the skin, were first implanted by Michel Mirowski and colleagues in 1980 and shock the heart automatically when needed. When someone collapses in cardiac arrest, calling emergency services, starting CPR and using an AED within minutes can double or triple their chance of survival.",
s:[["Cardiac arrest","In sudden cardiac arrest, the heart stops pumping effectively and the person collapses, stops breathing normally and loses consciousness. Without treatment, death follows within minutes. Cardiac arrest is different from a heart attack, though a heart attack can trigger it."],["Using an AED","An AED is designed for untrained people. After it is switched on, it gives spoken instructions: attach the pads to the bare chest, stand clear while it analyzes the rhythm and press the shock button if told. It will not shock a heart that does not need it."],["CPR","Chest compressions keep blood flowing to the brain until a defibrillator arrives. Guidelines recommend pushing hard and fast in the center of the chest, about 100 to 120 times a minute."],["Public access","Many cities register AED locations so emergency call takers and apps can direct bystanders to the nearest one. Some countries require AEDs in schools and large public buildings."]],
k:[["First successful human defibrillation","Claude Beck, 1947"],["Portable defibrillator","Frank Pantridge, 1965"],["Implantable defibrillator","First implanted 1980"],["Main rhythm treated","Ventricular fibrillation"],["Public device","Automated external defibrillator (AED)"]],
d:[["1899","Scientists stop and restart animal hearts with electricity"],["1947","First successful human defibrillation"],["1960","Modern CPR technique described"],["1965","Portable defibrillator used in Belfast"],["1980","First implantable defibrillator"],["1990s","AEDs placed in public places"]],
s2:[["Frank Pantridge","Pantridge’s first portable defibrillator weighed about 70 kilograms and ran on car batteries. He later developed smaller versions; a statue of him stands in Lisburn, Northern Ireland."],["Not like the movies","In films, doctors often shock a heart with a flat line on the monitor, but defibrillators do not restart a completely stopped heart; that condition, called asystole, is treated with CPR and medicines."]],
sp:["A song for CPR","Some first-aid trainers suggest doing chest compressions to the beat of a song with about 100 to 120 beats per minute to keep the right pace."],
g:[["Defibrillator","A device that shocks the heart to restore rhythm"],["Ventricular fibrillation","A deadly quivering of the heart"],["AED","Automated external defibrillator"],["CPR","Cardiopulmonary resuscitation"]],
f:["The first successful human defibrillation was in 1947.","AEDs give spoken instructions.","CPR plus an AED improves survival."],
fun:"The first portable defibrillator weighed about 70 kilograms and ran on car batteries.",
tl:"Gumagamit ang defibrillator ng kuryente upang ihinto ang mapanganib na ritmo ng puso. Kahit sino ay maaaring gumamit ng AED dahil ginagabayan nito ang bawat hakbang, at malaki ang naitutulong ng mabilis na CPR at shock."});

ADDART({id:"dnasequencing",t:"DNA Sequencing",c:"Technology",i:"🧬",w:"DNA sequencing",r:["dna","genetherapy","pandemic","evo","cell"],
p:[["Reading the code of life","DNA sequencing reads the order of letters in DNA."],["The Human Genome Project","Scientists read nearly all human DNA by 2003."],["Faster and cheaper","Sequencing a genome now costs a few hundred dollars."]],
l:"DNA sequencing is the process of determining the exact order of the four chemical bases, adenine (A), cytosine (C), guanine (G) and thymine (T), that make up a DNA molecule. This order carries the genetic instructions of every living thing. In 1977, British biochemist Frederick Sanger developed a practical method, now called Sanger sequencing, which earned him his second Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Automated machines followed, and the international Human Genome Project, running from 1990 to 2003 at a cost of about 2.7 billion dollars, read almost all of the roughly 3 billion letters in human DNA. From 2005, ‘next-generation’ sequencing machines read millions of DNA fragments at once, and costs fell faster than computer prices: by the 2020s, sequencing a whole human genome cost a few hundred dollars and took about a day. In 2022, scientists published the first truly complete human genome, filling gaps left in 2003. Sequencing is used to diagnose genetic diseases, choose cancer treatments, trace ancestry, identify criminals and victims, track the spread of viruses such as SARS-CoV-2 and study evolution, from ancient Neanderthal bones to newly discovered microbes.",
s:[["Sanger sequencing","Sanger’s method copies DNA using building blocks that stop the copy at specific letters, creating fragments of different lengths that reveal the sequence. It is accurate and still used to check results, but it reads only one fragment at a time."],["Next-generation sequencing","Modern machines read billions of short fragments in parallel, and computers assemble them like a jigsaw puzzle by matching overlaps. Nanopore sequencers, some as small as a phone, read long DNA strands as they pass through tiny holes."],["Medicine","Sequencing helps doctors diagnose rare diseases in sick newborns within days, choose drugs targeting cancer mutations and screen for inherited risks. Some countries are sequencing newborns and large parts of their populations."],["Tracking viruses","During the COVID-19 pandemic, scientists sequenced millions of virus samples to identify new variants such as Delta and Omicron and share data worldwide in near real time."]],
k:[["DNA letters","A, C, G and T"],["Sanger sequencing","1977"],["Human Genome Project","1990–2003"],["Human genome size","About 3 billion letters"],["First complete human genome","2022"]],
d:[["1953","Structure of DNA described"],["1977","Sanger sequencing developed"],["1990","Human Genome Project begins"],["2003","Human Genome Project completed"],["2005","Next-generation sequencing machines"],["2022","First complete human genome published"]],
s2:[["Frederick Sanger","Sanger won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry twice: in 1958 for determining the structure of insulin and in 1980 for DNA sequencing. He is one of only a few people to win two Nobel Prizes."],["Ancient DNA","Swedish scientist Svante Pääbo sequenced the Neanderthal genome, showing that many people today carry some Neanderthal DNA. He received the Nobel Prize in 2022."]],
sp:["A library of letters","Printed out, the 3 billion letters of one human genome would fill about 200 books the size of a thick telephone directory."],
g:[["Sequencing","Reading the order of DNA bases"],["Genome","All the DNA in an organism"],["Base","One of the four DNA letters"],["Variant","A different version of a virus or gene"]],
f:["Sanger sequencing was developed in 1977.","The Human Genome Project finished in 2003.","Sequencing a genome now costs a few hundred dollars."],
fun:"Reading the first human genome cost billions of dollars; today it costs a few hundred dollars.",
tl:"Binabasa ng DNA sequencing ang pagkakasunod-sunod ng mga titik sa DNA. Nabasa ng mga siyentipiko ang halos lahat ng DNA ng tao noong 2003, at ilang daang dolyar na lamang ngayon ang gastos sa pag-sequence ng isang genome."});

ADDART({id:"verticalfarming",t:"Vertical Farming and Hydroponics",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🥬",w:"Vertical farming",r:["agri","plantsenses","leds","urban","waterchem"],
p:[["Farms indoors","Vertical farms grow crops in stacked layers inside buildings."],["No soil needed","Hydroponics grows plants in nutrient-rich water."],["Less water, more energy","They save water and land but use lots of electricity."]],
l:"Vertical farming is the practice of growing crops in stacked layers indoors, often in warehouses, shipping containers or skyscrapers, under carefully controlled light, temperature and humidity. Most vertical farms use hydroponics, growing plants without soil in water containing dissolved nutrients; the term was popularized by American scientist William Gericke in the 1930s. Related methods include aeroponics, in which roots hang in the air and are sprayed with nutrient mist, a technique studied by NASA for growing food in space, and aquaponics, which combines fish farming with plant growing. Modern vertical farms use LED lights tuned to the colors plants use most, sensors and automation. Supporters say vertical farms can grow food close to city consumers year-round, use up to 95 percent less water than field farming, need no pesticides and are unaffected by weather. American ecologist Dickson Despommier promoted the idea of farm skyscrapers in the 2000s. However, lighting and climate control use large amounts of electricity, so vertical farms mainly grow high-value leafy greens and herbs, and several large companies went out of business in the 2020s as energy costs rose. Singapore, Japan, the Netherlands and Gulf states have invested in the technology.",
s:[["Hydroponics","Plants need water, nutrients, light and carbon dioxide, not soil itself. In hydroponic systems, roots sit in flowing nutrient solution or in materials such as rock wool or coconut fiber. Many tomatoes and cucumbers in greenhouses are grown this way."],["LED lighting","LEDs can be tuned to emit mostly red and blue light, which plants use for photosynthesis, giving vertical farms their pink-purple glow. LEDs are far more efficient than older grow lamps."],["Advantages","Indoor farms can produce crops many times higher per square meter than fields, recycle water and avoid pests and storms. They suit places with little farmland or harsh climates, such as deserts or Arctic towns."],["Challenges","Electricity can be the largest cost, and if it comes from fossil fuels, the carbon footprint can be high. Staple crops such as wheat and rice are not practical to grow indoors at scale."]],
k:[["Main method","Hydroponics"],["Term ‘hydroponics’ popularized","William Gericke, 1930s"],["Water savings","Up to 95% less than field farming"],["Typical crops","Leafy greens and herbs"],["Main challenge","High energy use"]],
d:[["1930s","Gericke popularizes hydroponics"],["1940s","Hydroponic gardens feed troops on Pacific islands"],["1999","Despommier’s students develop the vertical farm idea"],["2012","Early commercial vertical farm opens in Singapore"],["2015","Astronauts eat lettuce grown on the ISS"]],
s2:[["Farms in space","Astronauts on the International Space Station grew and ate red romaine lettuce in 2015 in the Veggie plant chamber, using LED lights, an experiment that helps plan food for missions to Mars."],["Pacific gardens","During World War II, the US military used hydroponics to grow fresh vegetables for troops on barren Pacific islands such as Wake Island."]],
sp:["Pink farms","The mix of red and blue LED light makes many vertical farms glow pink or purple, an eerie sight compared with green fields."],
g:[["Hydroponics","Growing plants in nutrient water without soil"],["Aeroponics","Growing plants with roots sprayed by mist"],["Aquaponics","Combining fish farming and hydroponics"],["Vertical farm","An indoor farm with stacked growing layers"]],
f:["Vertical farms stack crops indoors.","Hydroponics grows plants without soil.","Astronauts ate space-grown lettuce in 2015."],
fun:"Vertical farms often glow pink because plants grow best under red and blue light.",
tl:"Nagtatanim ang vertical farm ng mga pananim sa patong-patong na hanay sa loob ng gusali. Hindi nangangailangan ng lupa ang hydroponics, at nakatitipid ito ng tubig at lupa ngunit malakas sa kuryente."});

ADDART({id:"labgrownmeat",t:"Lab-Grown Meat",c:"Food and Agriculture",i:"🧫",w:"Cultured meat",r:["plantbased","cattle","cell","climate","foodsecurity"],
p:[["Meat from cells","Cultivated meat is grown from animal cells instead of raised animals."],["The first burger","The first lab-grown burger was cooked in London in 2013."],["Approved in a few countries","Singapore, the United States and a few others allow its sale."]],
l:"Lab-grown meat, also called cultivated or cultured meat, is real animal meat produced by growing animal cells in large tanks called bioreactors rather than by raising and slaughtering animals. Scientists take a small sample of cells from a living animal and feed them nutrients so they multiply and form muscle and fat tissue. On 5 August 2013, Dutch scientist Mark Post presented the first lab-grown beef burger at a news event in London; it had cost about 250,000 euros to make. Supporters say cultivated meat could reduce animal suffering, use less land and water and cut greenhouse gas emissions from livestock, which account for a significant share of global emissions. In December 2020, Singapore became the first country to approve the sale of cultivated meat, chicken made by the US company Eat Just. The United States approved cultivated chicken from Upside Foods and Good Meat in 2023, and Israel and Australia approved products in 2024 and 2025. However, costs remain high, production is small, and questions remain about energy use and growth media. Some places, including Italy and several US states such as Florida and Alabama, have banned its sale, saying they want to protect traditional farming.",
s:[["How it is made","Cells are grown in a nutrient broth containing sugars, amino acids, vitamins and growth factors, inside sterile bioreactors similar to those used for brewing or making medicines. To make steaks rather than minced meat, cells must grow on scaffolds that give structure."],["Costs","The first burger cost hundreds of thousands of euros. Costs have fallen greatly, but making cultivated meat as cheap as ordinary meat at large scale remains a major challenge, requiring huge bioreactors and cheaper growth ingredients."],["Environmental questions","Studies suggest cultivated meat could greatly reduce land use and animal welfare problems. Its climate benefit depends on how much energy production uses and whether that energy is clean."],["Bans and debates","Italy banned cultivated meat in 2023, and some US states passed bans in 2024 and 2025. Supporters say consumers should be able to choose, while critics question safety, naturalness and the effect on farmers."]],
k:[["First lab-grown burger","5 August 2013, London"],["First country to approve sale","Singapore, December 2020"],["US approval","2023"],["Made in","Bioreactors"],["Banned in","Italy and some US states"]],
d:[["2013","First lab-grown burger presented"],["2020","Singapore approves cultivated chicken"],["2023","US approves cultivated chicken; Italy bans cultivated meat"],["2024","Israel approves cultivated beef; Florida and Alabama ban it"],["2025","Australia approves cultivated quail; UK approves cultivated pet food"]],
s2:[["Winston Churchill’s prediction","In 1931, Winston Churchill predicted that people would one day ‘escape the absurdity of growing a whole chicken’ just to eat parts of it, by growing the parts separately."],["Plant-based meat","Plant-based meat substitutes, made from soy, peas and other plants, are a different approach to reducing meat from animals and are already widely sold."]],
sp:["The first taste","Food critics who tasted the 2013 burger said it was close to meat but lacked fat and juiciness, because it contained only muscle cells."],
g:[["Cultivated meat","Meat grown from animal cells"],["Bioreactor","A tank for growing cells"],["Growth medium","The nutrient broth cells grow in"],["Scaffold","A structure that cells grow on"]],
f:["The first lab-grown burger was cooked in 2013.","Singapore approved cultivated meat first, in 2020.","Italy banned cultivated meat in 2023."],
fun:"The first lab-grown burger, cooked in 2013, cost about 250,000 euros to make.",
tl:"Pinalalaki ang cultivated meat mula sa selula ng hayop sa halip na mag-alaga ng hayop. Niluto ang unang lab-grown burger sa London noong 2013, at pinapayagan na ang pagbebenta nito sa Singapore, United States at ilang iba pang bansa."});

ADDART({id:"hotairballoons",t:"Hot-Air Balloons",c:"Technology",i:"🎈",w:"Hot-air balloon",r:["flight","airships","thermo","weatherforecast","francecountry"],
p:[["The first human flight","People first flew in a hot-air balloon in 1783."],["Hot air rises","Heating the air inside makes the balloon lighter than the air around it."],["Around the world","A balloon first flew nonstop around the world in 1999."]],
l:"A hot-air balloon is an aircraft consisting of a large fabric bag, called an envelope, with a basket underneath and a burner that heats the air inside. Because hot air is less dense than the cooler air around it, the balloon floats upward. Pilots climb by heating the air and descend by letting it cool or releasing hot air through a vent; they steer by rising or sinking to find winds blowing in different directions. The hot-air balloon was invented by the French brothers Joseph-Michel and Jacques-Étienne Montgolfier, paper manufacturers, who demonstrated an uncrewed balloon on 4 June 1783 in Annonay. In September 1783, they flew a sheep, a duck and a rooster at Versailles before King Louis XVI. On 21 November 1783, Jean-François Pilâtre de Rozier and the Marquis d’Arlandes made the first free flight by humans, drifting over Paris for about 25 minutes. Days later, a hydrogen balloon carried Jacques Charles aloft. Modern hot-air balloons, using propane burners and nylon envelopes, were developed by American Ed Yost around 1960. In March 1999, Bertrand Piccard and Brian Jones made the first nonstop balloon flight around the world in the Breitling Orbiter 3, a combined helium and hot-air balloon.",
s:[["How balloons fly","A typical balloon envelope holds about 2,800 cubic meters of air heated to about 100°C. Balloons cannot be steered directly; pilots use weather forecasts and change altitude to catch winds in the direction they want to go. Flights usually take place in the calm hours after sunrise or before sunset."],["Animal passengers","Before risking people, the Montgolfiers sent up a sheep, a duck and a rooster to test whether living creatures could survive at altitude. All landed safely, though the rooster was reportedly injured, perhaps by the sheep."],["Ballooning today","Hot-air ballooning is a popular sport and tourist activity. The Albuquerque International Balloon Fiesta in New Mexico gathers hundreds of balloons each October, and balloon rides over the rock formations of Cappadocia in Turkey are famous."],["Science balloons","Huge helium balloons carry telescopes and instruments to the edge of space, about 40 kilometers up, to study cosmic rays, the atmosphere and the stars."]],
k:[["Inventors","Montgolfier brothers, France"],["First public demonstration","4 June 1783"],["First human flight","21 November 1783, Paris"],["Modern propane balloon","Ed Yost, about 1960"],["First nonstop flight around the world","Breitling Orbiter 3, March 1999"]],
d:[["1783","Montgolfier balloon demonstrated in Annonay"],["1783","Sheep, duck and rooster fly at Versailles"],["1783","First human flight over Paris"],["1960","Ed Yost flies a modern hot-air balloon"],["1999","First nonstop balloon flight around the world"],["2002","Steve Fossett makes the first solo balloon flight around the world"]],
s2:[["Pilâtre de Rozier","Jean-François Pilâtre de Rozier, the first person to fly, also became one of the first people to die in an air crash, in 1785, when his combined hydrogen and hot-air balloon caught fire while attempting to cross the English Channel."],["Champagne tradition","Early balloonists in France carried champagne to calm frightened farmers where they landed, and many balloon pilots still toast passengers after a flight."]],
sp:["Piccard family","Bertrand Piccard’s grandfather Auguste Piccard flew a balloon into the stratosphere in 1931, and his father Jacques Piccard dived to the deepest point of the ocean in 1960."],
g:[["Envelope","The fabric bag of a balloon"],["Burner","The device that heats the air"],["Buoyancy","The upward force on something lighter than its surroundings"],["Propane","A gas fuel used by balloon burners"]],
f:["People first flew in a balloon in 1783.","Hot air rises because it is less dense.","A balloon first circled the world nonstop in 1999."],
fun:"The first passengers in a hot-air balloon were a sheep, a duck and a rooster.",
tl:"Unang lumipad ang tao sa hot-air balloon noong 1783. Umaangat ang lobo dahil gumagaan ito kapag pinainit ang hangin sa loob, at unang nakaikot nang walang tigil sa mundo ang isang lobo noong 1999."});

ADDART({id:"parachutes",t:"Parachutes",c:"Technology",i:"🪂",w:"Parachute",r:["flight","grav","hotairballoons","marsrovers","spacesuits"],
p:[["Slowing a fall","Parachutes use air resistance to slow falling people and objects."],["The first jump","André-Jacques Garnerin made the first parachute jump from a balloon in 1797."],["From skydivers to spacecraft","Parachutes land spacecraft on Earth and Mars."]],
l:"A parachute is a device made of fabric that creates drag, or air resistance, to slow the fall of a person or object through the air. Leonardo da Vinci sketched a pyramid-shaped parachute around 1485, and in 2000 a British skydiver showed that a version built from his design could work. The first recorded parachute jump from a great height was made on 22 October 1797 by Frenchman André-Jacques Garnerin, who jumped from a hydrogen balloon over Paris using a silk parachute. In 1911, Russian inventor Gleb Kotelnikov designed a parachute packed in a backpack, and parachutes for airplane pilots spread after World War I; few pilots in that war had them. Paratroopers were dropped into battle during World War II. Today, parachutes are used for the sport of skydiving, emergency escape from aircraft, dropping supplies and returning spacecraft to Earth. Rectangular ram-air parachutes, which act like wings and can be steered, replaced many round canopies. Special parachutes have slowed probes landing on Mars and Saturn’s moon Titan. On 14 October 2012, Felix Baumgartner jumped from a balloon at about 39 kilometers, breaking the sound barrier in free fall before opening his parachute.",
s:[["How parachutes work","A falling body speeds up until air resistance balances gravity, at a speed called terminal velocity, about 200 kilometers per hour for a skydiver falling belly-down. Opening a parachute greatly increases air resistance, slowing the descent to about 20 kilometers per hour for a safe landing."],["Skydiving","Skydivers jump from planes at about 4,000 meters, free-fall for about a minute and open their parachutes at around 1,000 meters. They carry a reserve parachute, and automatic devices can open it if the skydiver does not."],["Parachutes in space","Space capsules such as Apollo, Soyuz, Crew Dragon and Orion use several parachutes to slow down after reentry. NASA’s Perseverance rover used a supersonic parachute 21.5 meters wide, which carried a hidden coded message in its pattern."],["Record jumps","In 2014, Google executive Alan Eustace jumped from about 41 kilometers, breaking Baumgartner’s altitude record, using a balloon and a specially designed pressure suit."]],
k:[["First sketch","Leonardo da Vinci, about 1485"],["First jump from a balloon","André-Jacques Garnerin, 22 October 1797"],["Backpack parachute","Gleb Kotelnikov, 1911"],["Skydiver terminal velocity","About 200 km/h"],["Highest jumps","Baumgartner (2012), Eustace (2014)"]],
d:[["c. 1485","Leonardo sketches a parachute"],["1797","Garnerin’s parachute jump over Paris"],["1911","Kotelnikov’s backpack parachute"],["1919","Leslie Irvin makes a free-fall jump with a ripcord parachute"],["2012","Felix Baumgartner jumps from 39 kilometers"],["2021","Perseverance’s parachute carries a hidden message"]],
s2:[["Perseverance’s secret message","The orange and white pattern of Perseverance’s parachute spelled ‘Dare Mighty Things’, a motto of NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory, in binary code, along with the lab’s coordinates. Internet users decoded it within hours."],["The Caterpillar Club","People whose lives were saved by a silk parachute bailing out of an aircraft can join the Caterpillar Club, named after the silkworms that produced the silk."]],
sp:["Leonardo’s design works","In 2000, British skydiver Adrian Nicholas descended safely under a parachute built of canvas and wood following Leonardo da Vinci’s 500-year-old sketch."],
g:[["Drag","Air resistance slowing motion"],["Terminal velocity","The steady top speed of a falling object"],["Canopy","The fabric part of a parachute"],["Ripcord","A cord pulled to open a parachute"]],
f:["Garnerin made the first parachute jump from a balloon in 1797.","Parachutes land spacecraft.","Perseverance’s parachute hid a coded message."],
fun:"The pattern on the Mars rover Perseverance’s parachute hid the message ‘Dare Mighty Things’ in binary code.",
tl:"Gumagamit ang parachute ng resistensiya ng hangin upang pabagalin ang pagbagsak ng tao at bagay. Si André-Jacques Garnerin ang unang tumalon gamit ang parachute mula sa lobo noong 1797, at ginagamit din ito sa paglapag ng spacecraft sa Earth at Mars."});

ADDART({id:"subways",t:"Subways and Metros",c:"Technology",i:"🚇",w:"Rapid transit",r:["rail","tunnels","urban","ukcountry","elec"],
p:[["Trains under the city","Subways are rapid transit railways, often underground."],["London first","The world’s first underground railway opened in London in 1863."],["Moving millions","Metro systems carry millions of passengers in big cities every day."]],
l:"A subway, also called a metro, underground or rapid transit system, is an urban railway that carries large numbers of passengers on frequent trains, usually running in tunnels or on elevated tracks separated from street traffic. The world’s first underground railway, London’s Metropolitan Railway, opened on 10 January 1863, using steam locomotives in shallow tunnels dug by cutting trenches under streets and covering them. In 1890, London opened the first deep-level electric ‘tube’ line. Budapest opened continental Europe’s first underground railway in 1896, Boston the first American subway in 1897, Paris its Métro in 1900 and New York its subway in 1904. Tokyo’s first subway line opened in 1927, Asia’s first. Moscow’s Metro, opened in 1935, became famous for its palace-like stations with chandeliers and mosaics. Today, more than 200 cities have metro systems, and China has built more than any other country, with Shanghai and Beijing each operating networks of more than 800 kilometers. Metros reduce traffic and pollution because one train can carry over a thousand people. Many new lines are driverless, controlled by computers.",
s:[["Building tunnels","Early lines used ‘cut and cover’, digging up streets and roofing over the trench. Deeper tunnels are bored by tunneling shields and modern tunnel boring machines, which can dig through rock and soil while lining the tunnel with concrete segments."],["Maps","Harry Beck’s map of the London Underground, designed in 1931, ignored real distances and showed lines as straight colored routes with neat angles, making it easy to read. Its style was copied by transit maps worldwide."],["Driverless trains","Fully automated metros, such as those in Copenhagen, Dubai, Singapore and Paris Line 14, run without drivers, allowing frequent trains around the clock. Platform screen doors keep passengers safe."],["Metros in Southeast Asia","Manila’s Light Rail Transit, opened in 1984, was the first rapid transit line in Southeast Asia. Singapore, Bangkok, Kuala Lumpur and Jakarta have since built extensive networks."]],
k:[["First underground railway","London, 10 January 1863"],["First deep electric tube","London, 1890"],["First in Asia","Tokyo, 1927"],["Largest networks","Shanghai and Beijing"],["Cities with metros","More than 200"]],
d:[["1863","London’s Metropolitan Railway opens"],["1890","First deep electric tube line"],["1896","Budapest’s underground opens"],["1904","New York City Subway opens"],["1927","Tokyo subway opens"],["1935","Moscow Metro opens"],["1984","Manila’s LRT opens"]],
s2:[["Mind the gap","The London Underground’s recorded warning ‘Mind the gap’, reminding passengers to step over the space between train and platform, became a famous phrase printed on souvenirs."],["Shelters in war","During World War II, Londoners sheltered from bombing raids in Underground stations, and metros in Kyiv and other cities have served as shelters in recent conflicts."]],
sp:["Busiest system","Tokyo’s rail and metro networks are among the busiest in the world, with ‘pushers’ at some stations once famous for helping squeeze passengers onto crowded trains."],
g:[["Metro","An urban rapid transit railway"],["Cut and cover","Building a tunnel by digging a trench and roofing it"],["Tunnel boring machine","A machine that digs tunnels"],["Platform screen doors","Doors separating platforms from tracks"]],
f:["The first underground railway opened in London in 1863.","Tokyo opened Asia’s first subway in 1927.","Shanghai has one of the largest metro networks."],
fun:"The first underground trains in London were pulled by steam locomotives in tunnels under the streets.",
tl:"Ang subway ay mabilis na tren sa lungsod na madalas tumatakbo sa ilalim ng lupa. Binuksan sa London noong 1863 ang unang underground railway sa mundo, at milyun-milyong pasahero ang dinadala araw-araw ng mga metro sa malalaking lungsod."});

ADDART({id:"biometrics",t:"Fingerprints and Biometrics",c:"Technology",i:"🖐️",w:"Biometrics",r:["facialrecognition","passwords","smartphone","cybersecurity","eye"],
p:[["Your body as an ID","Biometrics identifies people by fingerprints, faces, eyes or voices."],["Fingerprints solved crimes","Fingerprints were first used to solve a murder in 1892."],["Unlocking phones","Phones use fingerprint and face scanners instead of passwords."]],
l:"Biometrics is the measurement of physical or behavioral characteristics to identify people. Fingerprints are the best-known example: the patterns of ridges on fingertips form before birth and are unique to each person, even identical twins. People in ancient Babylon and China pressed fingerprints into clay or documents as signatures. In the 1880s, Scottish doctor Henry Faulds suggested using fingerprints to identify criminals, and English scientist Francis Galton showed they were unique and permanent. In 1892, Argentine police officer Juan Vucetich used a bloody fingerprint to solve a murder, the first such case. Scotland Yard adopted a fingerprint classification system in 1901. Other biometrics include face, iris, retina, voice, hand shape, vein patterns, walking style and typing rhythm. Iris recognition, using patterns in the colored part of the eye, was developed by John Daugman in the 1990s. Apple’s Touch ID (2013) and Face ID (2017) made biometrics part of daily life. Biometric passports, airport gates and national ID systems, such as India’s Aadhaar with more than a billion people enrolled, use them widely. Critics warn about privacy, surveillance, errors and the fact that, unlike a password, a fingerprint cannot be changed if stolen.",
s:[["How fingerprint scanners work","Scanners capture an image of the ridges using light, electrical charge or ultrasound. Software identifies features such as ridge endings and splits, called minutiae, and compares them with stored patterns. Phones store this data securely on the device rather than as a photo."],["Before fingerprints","In the 1880s, French police officer Alphonse Bertillon identified criminals by measuring body parts such as head length and arm span. The system was abandoned after fingerprinting proved more reliable."],["Iris and face","The iris has complex patterns that stay stable for decades. Face recognition is convenient but can be less accurate and has shown higher error rates for some groups in tests, raising concerns about fairness."],["Privacy","Because biometric data is permanent, its theft or misuse is serious. The European Union’s data protection law treats biometric data as sensitive, and some cities have restricted government use of face recognition."]],
k:[["First murder solved with fingerprints","Argentina, 1892"],["Scotland Yard fingerprint system","1901"],["Iris recognition","John Daugman, 1990s"],["Apple Touch ID","2013"],["India’s Aadhaar","More than 1 billion enrolled"]],
d:[["1880","Henry Faulds publishes on fingerprints"],["1892","Galton’s book on fingerprints; Vucetich solves a murder"],["1901","Scotland Yard adopts fingerprinting"],["1990s","Iris recognition developed"],["2010","India begins issuing Aadhaar numbers"],["2013","Touch ID introduced"],["2017","Face ID introduced"]],
s2:[["Koalas have fingerprints","Koalas have fingerprints so similar to humans’ that, in theory, they could confuse crime scene investigators."],["Never the same","Even identical twins, who share the same DNA, have different fingerprints, because tiny differences in conditions in the womb shape the ridges."]],
sp:["Fingerprint ridges","Fingerprint ridges may help people grip objects and sense textures, by making skin vibrate as fingers slide across surfaces."],
g:[["Biometrics","Identifying people by body features"],["Minutiae","Small details in fingerprint ridges"],["Iris","The colored part of the eye"],["Enrollment","Registering someone’s biometric data"]],
f:["Fingerprints are unique, even for identical twins.","Fingerprints solved a murder in 1892.","Phones use fingerprint and face scanners."],
fun:"Koalas have fingerprints so similar to humans’ that they could confuse investigators.",
tl:"Kinikilala ng biometrics ang tao sa pamamagitan ng fingerprint, mukha, mata o boses. Unang ginamit ang fingerprint upang lutasin ang isang pagpatay noong 1892, at gumagamit na ang mga telepono ng fingerprint at face scanner sa halip na password."});

ADDART({id:"welding",t:"Welding",c:"Technology",i:"👨‍🏭",w:"Welding",r:["metals","steel","robot","ships","cars"],
p:[["Joining metal","Welding joins pieces of metal by melting them together."],["Electric arcs","Arc welding uses an electric spark hotter than the Sun’s surface."],["Ships, cars and skyscrapers","Modern construction depends on welding."]],
l:"Welding is a process for joining materials, usually metals, by heating them so that they melt and fuse together, often with an added filler metal, forming a joint as strong as the original material. Ancient blacksmiths practiced forge welding, heating iron until soft and hammering pieces together; the famous Iron Pillar of Delhi, about 1,600 years old, was made this way. Modern welding began in the 19th century with electricity. Russian inventor Nikolay Benardos patented carbon arc welding in the 1880s, using an electric arc, a continuous spark between an electrode and the metal that reaches temperatures of several thousand degrees Celsius. Gas welding with an oxygen and acetylene flame developed around 1903. Welding was used extensively in shipbuilding during World War II, when American shipyards built thousands of welded cargo ships called Liberty ships much faster than riveted ones. Today, welding joins car bodies, pipelines, bridges, skyscraper frames, ships and spacecraft. Robots perform much of the welding in car factories, and lasers, electron beams and friction are also used. Welders wear protective helmets with dark visors because the arc’s intense light can damage eyes.",
s:[["Types of welding","Common methods include shielded metal arc (stick) welding, MIG welding, which feeds a wire electrode through a gun, and TIG welding, which uses a tungsten electrode for precise work. Spot welding joins sheet metal with short electrical pulses, as in car bodies."],["Liberty ships","Welded Liberty ships could be built in weeks; one was assembled in under five days in 1942. Some early ships cracked in cold water, which led scientists to study how steel becomes brittle at low temperatures."],["Robot welders","In 1961, General Motors installed Unimate, the first industrial robot, at a factory in New Jersey, where it handled hot metal parts; robots soon took over much spot welding on car assembly lines."],["Welding in space","In 1969, Soviet cosmonauts on Soyuz 6 performed the first welding experiments in space. In a vacuum, clean metal surfaces can even stick together without heat, a phenomenon called cold welding."]],
k:[["Ancient method","Forge welding"],["Carbon arc welding","Nikolay Benardos, 1880s"],["Gas welding","About 1903"],["Arc temperature","Several thousand °C"],["First welding in space","Soyuz 6, 1969"]],
d:[["c. 400","Iron Pillar of Delhi made by forge welding"],["1881","Benardos demonstrates carbon arc welding"],["1903","Oxy-acetylene welding developed"],["1941","Liberty ship program begins"],["1961","Unimate robot installed at General Motors"],["1969","First welding in space on Soyuz 6"]],
s2:[["Underwater welding","Divers weld underwater to repair ships, oil platforms and pipelines, using special electrodes and techniques. It is one of the most dangerous welding jobs."],["Welding sparks and eyes","Looking at a welding arc without protection can cause ‘arc eye’, a painful burn of the cornea, like sunburn of the eye. Automatic welding helmets darken instantly when the arc starts."]],
sp:["Hotter than the Sun","A welding arc can reach temperatures higher than the surface of the Sun, about 5,500°C."],
g:[["Welding","Joining materials by melting them together"],["Electric arc","A continuous electric spark"],["Electrode","A conductor that carries current to the weld"],["Filler metal","Metal added to form a weld joint"]],
f:["Welding joins metal by melting it.","Liberty ships were welded during World War II.","The first welding in space was in 1969."],
fun:"In space, very clean metal surfaces can weld together just by touching, a process called cold welding.",
tl:"Pinagdurugtong ng welding ang mga metal sa pagtunaw sa mga ito. Gumagamit ang arc welding ng kislap ng kuryente na mas mainit pa sa ibabaw ng Araw, at nakasalalay sa welding ang paggawa ng barko, kotse at matataas na gusali."});

ADDART({id:"tidalpower",t:"Tidal Power",c:"Technology",i:"🌊",w:"Tidal power",r:["tides","renew","hydropower","moon","oceanenergy"],
p:[["Energy from tides","Tidal power uses the rise and fall of the sea to make electricity."],["Predictable power","Tides come and go like clockwork, unlike wind and sun."],["Few big plants","Only a few large tidal power stations exist."]],
l:"Tidal power is a form of renewable energy that converts the energy of the tides, the regular rise and fall of the sea caused mainly by the Moon’s gravity, into electricity. Unlike wind and solar power, tides are completely predictable years ahead. People used tide mills to grind grain in medieval Europe, trapping water at high tide and releasing it through a waterwheel. The first large tidal power station, La Rance in Brittany, France, opened in 1966 with a dam, called a barrage, across an estuary and turbines that generate about 240 megawatts as water flows in and out. South Korea’s Sihwa Lake station, opened in 2011 with 254 megawatts, is the largest. Newer tidal stream systems place underwater turbines, like submerged wind turbines, in fast tidal currents; the MeyGen project in Scotland’s Pentland Firth has generated electricity since 2016. Tidal power produces no greenhouse gases while operating, but suitable sites with large tidal ranges or strong currents are rare, construction in the sea is costly, and barrages can affect fish, birds and sediment. As a result, tidal energy provides only a tiny share of the world’s electricity.",
s:[["How tides work","The Moon’s gravity pulls the oceans into bulges on the sides of Earth facing toward and away from it, and as Earth rotates, most coasts experience two high tides and two low tides each day. The Sun’s gravity adds to the effect, making larger spring tides and smaller neap tides."],["Barrages and lagoons","A barrage traps water behind a dam at high tide and releases it through turbines, like a hydroelectric dam. Tidal lagoons would enclose part of a bay with walls, possibly reducing environmental impact."],["Tidal stream turbines","Tidal stream turbines spin in currents that can exceed 3 meters per second. Because water is about 800 times denser than air, even slow currents carry much energy, so tidal turbines can be smaller than wind turbines."],["The Bay of Fundy","The Bay of Fundy in Canada has the highest tides in the world, with a difference of up to about 16 meters between high and low tide. Several projects have tested tidal turbines there."]],
k:[["Main cause of tides","The Moon’s gravity"],["First large station","La Rance, France, 1966"],["Largest station","Sihwa Lake, South Korea (254 MW)"],["Tidal stream example","MeyGen, Scotland"],["Highest tides","Bay of Fundy, Canada"]],
d:[["c. 1100","Tide mills used in Europe"],["1966","La Rance tidal power station opens"],["1984","Annapolis Royal station opens in Nova Scotia"],["2011","Sihwa Lake station opens"],["2016","MeyGen tidal stream turbines begin generating"]],
s2:[["Old tide mills","Some medieval tide mills still survive, including the Woodbridge Tide Mill in England, which has operated in some form since the 12th century and still grinds flour for visitors."],["Wave power","Wave power, a different technology, captures energy from the motion of waves on the ocean surface. It is still mostly experimental."]],
sp:["Slowing Earth","Tides gradually slow Earth’s rotation, making days longer by about 2 milliseconds per century, and push the Moon about 3.8 centimeters farther away each year."],
g:[["Tidal power","Electricity from tides"],["Barrage","A dam across an estuary"],["Tidal stream","A fast current caused by tides"],["Tidal range","The height difference between high and low tide"]],
f:["Tides are caused mainly by the Moon.","La Rance opened in 1966.","Tidal power is very predictable."],
fun:"The tides slowly push the Moon about 3.8 centimeters farther from Earth every year.",
tl:"Ginagamit ng tidal power ang pagtaas at pagbaba ng dagat upang makagawa ng kuryente. Mahuhulaan ang pagdating ng tides, hindi gaya ng hangin at araw, ngunit iilan lamang ang malalaking tidal power station."});
